{"name":"Distilled Moss","hex":"#ccffcc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"distilled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having been produced by means of distillation.","Having been subjected to distillation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of distil","simple past and past participle of distill"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/distilled"},{"term":"moss","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various small, green, seedless plants growing on the ground or on the surfaces of trees, stones, etc.; now specifically, a plant of the phylum Bryophyta (formerly division Musci).","A kind or species of such plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with moss.","To cover (something) with moss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moss"}],"entries":[{"title":"Distillation","blocks":[{"text":"Distillation, also classical distillation, is the process of separating the component substances of a liquid mixture of two or more chemically discrete substances by selective boiling of the mixture and the condensation of the vapors in a still."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Simple_distillation_apparatus.svg/330px-Simple_distillation_apparatus.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distillation"},{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Zosimos_distillation_equipment.jpg/500px-Zosimos_distillation_equipment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Zosimos_distillation_equipment.jpg/500px-Zosimos_distillation_equipment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distillation equipment used by the 3rd-century alchemist Zosimos of Panopolis[13][14] (as imagined by the anonymous illustrator of the 15th-century Byzantine Greek manuscript Codex Parisinus graecus 2327).[15]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZosimos_distillation_equipment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Hieronymus_Brunschwig_Liber_de_arte_Distillandi_CHF_AQ13x3.jpg/500px-Hieronymus_Brunschwig_Liber_de_arte_Distillandi_CHF_AQ13x3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Hieronymus_Brunschwig_Liber_de_arte_Distillandi_CHF_AQ13x3.jpg/1280px-Hieronymus_Brunschwig_Liber_de_arte_Distillandi_CHF_AQ13x3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t